// An **enum literal with a payload** as an attribute argument keeps its enum name when displayed,
// exactly as a source-constructed variant does. The VM used to mark any payload-carrying attribute
// enum as a built-in `Result`/`Option` — the flag that *suppresses* the enum name — so
// `Mode.Fast(3)` reflected as `Fast(3)` in the VM and `Mode.Fast(3)` in the interpreter: a
// differential mismatch no corpus case had reached, because none carried a payload here.
// A payload-free variant, and a real `Result`/`Option`, keep rendering as they always did.
// expect: stdout "Mode.Fast(3)"
// expect: stdout "Mode.Slow"
// expect: stdout "Ok(1)"
// expect: exit 0
enum Mode { Fast(n: int); Slow }

@attribute(Function)
struct Cfg { mode: dyn }

#[Cfg(mode: Mode.Fast(3))]
fn fast(): void { return }

#[Cfg(mode: Mode.Slow)]
fn slow(): void { return }

for c in attributes_of::<Cfg>() {
    echo c.value.mode;
}
echo Ok(1);
